{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/builder-task.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Build a Freya phone agent from a bank's case folder\r\n\r\nYou are the builder. You read the bank's documents and produce ONE Freya workflow (a phone\r\nagent), save it, test it, fix it, and report. Work unattended. Nothing in this file describes\r\nany particular case: everything case-specific comes from the documents.\r\n\r\n## Run parameters (filled in by builder/build.py)\r\n\r\n- Sources (every case document converted to text, `BRIEF.md` and `README.md` first): `C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/sources`\r\n- Mock bank base URL (the team's deployed mock of the bank's services): `https://freya-mock-bank.konurozkan1905.workers.dev`\r\n- Workflow slot (updated in place): `final-r3`\r\n- Write the final workflow JSON to: `C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/workflow.json`\r\n- Write your result to: `C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/agent-result.json`  as JSON: {\"workflow_id\": ..., \"agent_id\": ..., \"tests\": \"<summary>\", \"decisions\": \"<path to your notes>\"}\r\n- Write design notes (conflicts found and how you resolved them) to: `C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/design-notes.md`\r\n- Python: `C:/Python312/python.exe` (run Freya helpers from the kit root, which is your working directory)\r\n- Time budget for your whole run: about 39 minutes. Plan for three or four test rounds;\r\n  start the first test as early as possible.\r\n\r\n## Steps\r\n\r\n1. Read every file in the sources folder. `BRIEF.md` says what to build; `README.md` says what\r\n   each document is. The documents were written at different times and can disagree: decide\r\n   which one is authoritative from their dates, versions, the mails and the meeting notes\r\n   (a later decision or an explicit amendment wins). Write each conflict and your decision to\r\n   the notes file.\r\n2. Read `docs/workflow-format.md`, `docs/tools-and-code.md` and `examples/base-agent.json`\r\n   (a small working agent; use it as the JSON pattern, not as the answer).\r\n3. From the documents, list: the variables every call provides (never ask the customer for\r\n   them), the bank services the agent needs (exact paths, methods, field names, request and\r\n   response shapes, error statuses), the call flow, the exact wording the bank requires, the\r\n   language of the calls, and the edge cases in the FAQ and example conversations.\r\n4. Write the workflow to the workflow path:\r\n   - Bank tools call `https://freya-mock-bank.konurozkan1905.workers.dev` + the documented path, with the documented method and field\r\n     names. No auth headers or secrets. `config.timeoutMs` 30000. `maxRetries` 0 for any\r\n     service that changes something (a write must never repeat).\r\n   - Speak the call's language in every spoken line. Prompts may be in English but must tell\r\n     the model to reply only in the call's language.\r\n   - Say only facts that a tool returned or the documents state. Never invent an amount, date,\r\n     card, address or status. Read amounts and dates the way the documents ask.\r\n   - Every date decision uses the call's date/time variable, never the system clock. Do date\r\n     arithmetic and other exact logic in a `code` node and give the model the finished facts.\r\n   - Never claim an action succeeded before its tool returned success. When a tool fails, say\r\n     so and follow the documented fallback (for example a handoff).\r\n   - Follow the documented flow order; route on tool results and clear customer intents.\r\n   - Cover EVERY branch: every service in the contract and every path in the flow drawing must\r\n     be reachable in the workflow. List them in the notes file and check each one off.\r\n   - After the main answer, offer and handle every follow-up the documents allow. A request that\r\n     one of the documented services can serve is NEVER out of scope: route it to that flow.\r\n     Only requests no documented service covers get the out-of-scope handling.\r\n   - Requests the documents say apply at ANY point (for example asking for a person, or other\r\n     documented global intents) must work from every step of the flow, including in the middle\r\n     of another branch: use global transitions for them, not per-step prompts.\r\n   - A clear no to a yes/no question is final: take the documented next step at once and never\r\n     ask the same question again. Only a hedged answer is asked again, as the documents say.\r\n   - When a branch says to tell the caller something from a service answer (for example a\r\n     related record or a status), read those fields in that branch.\r\n   - Never read back digits or identifiers beyond what the documents allow, even ones the\r\n     caller just said.\r\n   - Handle each documented error/warning code exactly as the documents say (the announcement\r\n     and where the call goes next). Do not collapse different codes into one generic handoff.\r\n   - Before ending, give the documented closing (thank and say goodbye), then end the call.\r\n   - Keep it robust: few, clear steps; short prompts; rules in routes and code rather than\r\n     long prompts. The phone agent model is Freya's own model, smaller than you.\r\n5. Validate without saving:\r\n   `C:/Python312/python.exe -c \"import freya,json,sys; print(json.dumps(freya.validate_workflow('check', json.load(open(sys.argv[1], encoding='utf-8')))))\" C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/workflow.json`\r\n   Fix every error it reports.\r\n6. Save (same slot updates the same agent):\r\n   `C:/Python312/python.exe -c \"import freya,json,sys; print(json.dumps(freya.save_workflow(sys.argv[1], json.load(open(sys.argv[2], encoding='utf-8')))))\" final-r3 C:/Users/Onur/Desktop/FreyaHackathon/build-the-builder/out/final-20261011-181035/workflow.json`\r\n   It prints `workflow_id` and `agent_id`.\r\n7. Test: write 8 to 12 scenarios to a JSON file next to the workflow, built from the documents'\r\n   example conversations and test customers (persona behaviour, success criteria, and the call\r\n   variables each call provides). At least one scenario per branch and per follow-up service,\r\n   plus error/warning answers from the test data. Then run, in the foreground, and wait (3 to 11 minutes):\r\n   `C:/Python312/python.exe tools/scenario_test.py <agent_id> <scenarios.json>`\r\n   Read each FAIL and its transcript, fix the workflow file, validate, save and test again.\r\n   Repeat until all pass or the time budget is nearly used. Always leave the last saved\r\n   workflow in a working state.\r\n8. Write the result file and finish with one line: workflow_id, agent_id, scenarios passed.\r\n\r\n## Rules\r\n\r\n- Read only: the sources folder, `docs/`, `examples/base-agent.json`, `freya.py` and\r\n  `tools/scenario_test.py`. Every other file in this repository (briefs, tests, out/, mock/,\r\n  public-test-set.json, builder code) is from earlier practice work and does not apply.\r\n- Never edit the agent on the platform by hand; change the file and save it.\r\n- Never print or copy secrets (API keys, tokens) into any file or message.\r\n- Do not change files under `builder/`, `tools/` or `docs/`: you build the agent, not the builder.\r\n- If something blocks you, write what and why to the notes file and still save the best\r\n  workflow you have.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
